.TH "REPROCTL\-HERDR" "1" "2026" "reproctl" "Repro Development Tools"
.SH NAME
reproctl\-herdr \- open the current checkout in a project\-scoped Herdr
session
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]reproctl herdr open\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Opens or reuses the current checkout as a Herdr workspace.
Repeated calls use the canonical checkout path to find an existing
workspace before opening one, and do not take focus from the current
workspace.
.PP
The repository\-owned \f[B].herdr/config.toml\f[R] is selected with
\f[B]HERDR_CONFIG_PATH\f[R].
A deterministic named Herdr session is derived from the canonical main
checkout.
This keeps Repro\(aqs settings separate from the default session and
does not modify the user\(aqs global Herdr config.
.PP
Herdr does not expose a supported query to verify the config loaded by
an already\-running server.
reproctl therefore reports the active server config as unverified.
It does not stop or restart an existing session or close unrelated
workspaces to apply the project settings.
Opening a checkout does not start Tilt services.
.PP
Issue\-based worktrees and \f[B]deliver\f[R] report a known Linear issue
title as \f[B]issue_title\f[R] metadata while retaining the issue\-ID
workspace label.
The title is truncated to Herdr\(aqs 80\-character metadata value limit
when needed.
.SH FAILURE RECOVERY
If the Herdr executable is missing, install it with:
.IP
.EX
brew install herdr
.EE
.PP
If the project\-scoped daemon is unavailable, run the foreground server
for only that named session with the config path and command printed by
reproctl, for example:
.IP
.EX
HERDR_CONFIG_PATH=\(dq/path/to/repo/.herdr/config.toml\(dq herdr \-\-session repro\-<24\-hex\-digits> server
.EE
.PP
The server remains in the foreground.
Then retry \f[B]reproctl herdr open\f[R] in another shell.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
reproctl herdr open
Open the current checkout in the project\(aqs named Herdr session.
.TP
reproctl setup \-\-open\-herdr
Run environment setup, then open the current checkout in Herdr.
